.\" version that of include/xisfconv.h: tests/run_tests.py holds both against the program.
.TH XISFCONV 1 "2026-10-09" "xisfconv 0.20.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xisfconv \- convert between PixInsight XISF, FITS and ASDF images; read DNG; export TIFF and PNG
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xisfconv
.RI [ options ]
.IR "file or directory" ...
.br
.B xisfconv \-\-verify
.IR "file or directory" ...
.br
.B xisfconv \-\-info
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xisfconv
converts astronomical images between the XISF format of PixInsight, FITS and ASDF, in every
direction, and writes TIFF and PNG pictures from all three. Keywords, the order of the rows,
astrometric solutions and XISF properties are carried from one format to the other. It also reads
the raw image of a DNG file (camera raw), to any of these formats.
.PP
The format of the input is recognized by its first bytes. The format of the output is given with
.BR \-t ,
or follows the name given with
.BR \-o .
Without either, an XISF file becomes a FITS file, and a FITS, ASDF or DNG file becomes an XISF file.
The output is named after the input, with the extension of its format, and is written next to it
unless
.B \-d
names a directory.
.PP
An XISF input is a monolithic file
.RI ( .xisf )
or the header file of a distributed unit
.RI ( .xish ),
whose data is in the files that header names
.RI ( .xisb ).
.B \-t xish
writes such a unit,
.B \-t xisf
one file. An XISF file given with
.BR "\-t xisf" ,
.B "\-t xish"
or
.B \-\-in\-place
is rewritten: its data blocks are stored another way (compressed, with checksums), and nothing else
changes.
.PP
Tile-compressed FITS
.RI ( .fits.fz ,
the format of
.BR fpack (1))
is read like any FITS file;
.B "\-t fits"
writes it as a plain FITS file, and
.B "\-t fits \-c"
writes a FITS file tile-compressed.
.PP
Of a DNG file the raw image is read: the samples of the sensor as the camera recorded them, cut to
the area that saw light and with the file's linearization table applied, and nothing else done to
them (not demosaiced, no black level taken off, no white balance). The colour filter pattern goes
to XISF as its ColorFilterArray element and, for a 2 x 2 RGB pattern, to the keyword
.BR BAYERPAT ;
the camera, the time and length of the exposure, the ISO speed and the focal length become
keywords. Uncompressed, lossless JPEG and Deflate compressed raw data are read; lossy DNG is not.
Nothing is written as DNG.
.PP
With several inputs, each is converted on its own; a file that fails is reported and the others
are still converted. No file is written twice in a run, and none over a file the run reads: of two
inputs with one output name the second is refused.
.PP
A directory stands for the image files in it and below it, by their names
.RI ( .xisf ,
.IR .xish ,
.IR .fits ,
.IR .fit ,
.IR .fts ,
.IR .fits.fz ,
.I .asdf
and
.IR .dng ),
in the order of the names. Of a directory, the files that are not yet what is written are
converted: with
.B "\-t fits"
the FITS files of a folder are passed over, which named alone would each be an error. With
.BR \-\-force ,
a file that is passed over is still replaced by the output of another file of its name
.RI ( frame.xisf
to
.IR frame.fits ).
Without
.BR \-t ,
a directory of one format is converted as a file of it is, and one that holds both XISF files and
FITS, ASDF or DNG files is refused, so that the direction is said and not guessed. Names that begin
with a dot are passed over, and links to directories are not followed.
.PP
An argument with
.B *
or
.B ?
that is not the name of something that is there is a pattern, and stands for the names it matches:
.B *
is any characters,
.B ?
exactly one. The program does this on every system, because the shells of Windows leave it to
the program; on Unix it applies to a pattern given in quotes. The wildcards may be in any part of
the path. A pattern that matches nothing is an error.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-to " \fIformat\fR"
The output format:
.BR fits ,
.BR asdf ,
.BR tiff ,
.BR png ,
.B xisf
or
.BR xish .
The default is
.B fits
for XISF input and
.B xisf
for FITS, ASDF and DNG input, or what the extension of the name given with
.B \-o
says.
.B xish
is XISF as a distributed unit: the header in
.IB name .xish
and the data blocks in
.IB name .xisb
beside it. (PixInsight itself opens monolithic
.I .xisf
files only.)
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIfile\fR"
The name of the output file, for a single input.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-outdir " \fIdir\fR"
The directory for the output files. The default is next to each input. The files of a directory
given as input keep their places below
.IR dir .
It is not searched for inputs where it lies inside a directory that is converted, or where a
pattern matches it.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite output files that exist.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-existing
Leave an output that exists as it is and pass its input over, without an error: a run on a
directory then converts what was added since the last one. Only the name is looked at, not the
dates.
.TP
.B \-\-in\-place
XISF to XISF: replace the input file. The new file is written next to it, read back and compared,
and only then takes its place. A file that is already stored as asked is left alone. Of a
directory, the XISF files are rewritten and the others passed over.
.SS Conversion
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bits " \fIformat\fR"
The sample format of the output:
.BR u8 ,
.BR u16 ,
.BR u32 ,
.B f32
or
.BR f64 .
The default is the format the input is stored in. Samples are rescaled, not cast.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-image " \fIn\fR"
Convert only image
.I n
(counted from 0). The default is all images: further images become further HDUs in FITS and ASDF,
and further pages in TIFF.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-compress
Compress the output. FITS: tile compression without loss
.RI ( \%image.fits.fz ),
.B RICE_1
for integers and
.B GZIP_2
for floating point; an output name that ends in
.I .fz
is written this way with or without the option. TIFF: Deflate with a predictor. XISF: the pixel
data, with Zstandard (zlib in a build without it) and byte shuffling; XISF to XISF: every attached
data block. ASDF: the pixel data, with zlib.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stretch [ =\fImode\fR ]
Apply a screen stretch, for looking at linear data.
.I mode
is
.B auto
(the default: the stretch PixInsight saved in the file if there is one, else
.BR linked ),
.B linked
(an automatic stretch with the statistics of the channels shared, which keeps the colour balance),
.B unlinked
(an automatic stretch for each channel, which takes colour casts out) or
.B stf
(only the stretch saved in the file). For FITS and ASDF input,
.B auto
means
.BR linked .
Stretched floating point data is written to TIFF as 16 bits unless
.B \-\-bits
says otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-bin " n"
TIFF and PNG output: a smaller picture,
.IR n " x " n
pixels averaged into one.
.TP
.BI \-\-resize " size"
TIFF and PNG output: a smaller picture, each of its pixels the mean of the pixels it covers.
.I size
is
.B 256
(the longest side in pixels),
.B 1024x768
(a box to fit, the proportions kept) or
.B 50%
(half the width and half the height). The picture is never larger than the image, and it is made
before a stretch is applied.
.TP
.B \-\-debayer
TIFF and PNG output: a colour picture of the mosaic of a one-shot colour camera, by bilinear
interpolation of its 2 x 2 colour filter pattern (that of the file, else
.B BAYERPAT
with
.B XBAYROFF
and
.BR YBAYROFF ),
before
.BR \-\-bin ,
.B \-\-resize
and a stretch. No white balance is applied. An image without such a pattern is written as it is,
with a warning. XISF, FITS and ASDF output keep the mosaic.
.TP
.B \-\-top\-down
XISF and DNG input: keep the top-down row order in FITS and ASDF output
.RB ( ROWORDER='TOP\-DOWN' )
in place of the FITS convention, bottom-up. FITS and ASDF input: the rows are stored top-down.
.TP
.B \-\-bottom\-up
FITS and ASDF input: the rows are stored bottom-up, whatever
.B ROWORDER
says.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-property\-keywords
From XISF: do not add the keywords that are missing and can be derived from XISF properties
.RB ( EXPTIME ,
.BR DATE\-OBS ,
.B BAYERPAT
and others).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-properties
From XISF: do not take the XISF properties along to FITS and ASDF. From FITS and ASDF: leave the
XISF properties a file carries where they are.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wcs
From XISF: do not write WCS keywords from an astrometric solution of PixInsight. To XISF: do not
write the solution properties of PixInsight from WCS keywords.
.TP
.BI \-\-sip\-order " n"
From XISF: the order of the SIP distortion polynomials that are fitted to the solution, 2 to 7.
The default is 3; 0 turns the fit off.
.TP
.BI \-\-bounds " lo:hi"
FITS and ASDF input: the range of floating point data. It is written as the bounds of the XISF
image, and taken as black and white for TIFF and PNG. The default is 0:1 if the data fits, else
0:65535 if it fits, else its minimum and maximum.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Do not verify the checksums of data blocks. XISF to XISF: do not read the output back either,
except with
.BR \-\-in\-place .
.TP
.BI \-\-external\-files " which"
XISF input: which files the header of a distributed unit may name for its data.
.B header\-dir
(the default): files in the directory of the header and below.
.BR anywhere :
also absolute paths,
.B file:
URLs and where symbolic links lead.
.BR none :
no file but the header itself. A header is data that came from somewhere, and could name any file
of the machine as the pixels of an image. Nothing is ever fetched from a network.
.SS XISF, ASDF and FITS output
.TP
.BI \-\-codec " codec"
The compression codec:
.BR zlib ,
.BR zstd ,
.BR lz4 ,
.B lz4hc
or
.BR none .
A codec implies
.BR \-\-compress .
XISF blocks are also byte shuffled (but with
.BR \-\-no\-shuffle );
.B lz4
and
.B lz4hc
are for XISF only.
.B zstd
in an ASDF file needs the asdf-compression package to be read in Python.
.B none
writes without compression; XISF to XISF it decompresses the blocks. FITS:
.B zlib
compresses the tiles of every sample type with gzip; there is no
.B zstd
or
.B lz4
for FITS.
.TP
.BI \-\-level " n"
XISF output: the compression level of the codec (implies
.BR \-\-compress ):
.B zlib
1 to 9 (6 if not given),
.B lz4hc
1 to 12 (9),
.B zstd
1 to 22 (3);
.B lz4
has none. A higher level makes a smaller file and takes longer to write. XISF to XISF: every
block is compressed again, since a file does not say with which level its blocks were.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-shuffle
XISF output: store the compressed blocks without byte shuffling (implies
.BR \-\-compress ).
Shuffling almost always makes them smaller.
.TP
.BI \-\-checksum " algorithm"
XISF: store a checksum of the pixel data block; XISF to XISF: of every attached block.
.I algorithm
is
.BR sha1 ,
.BR sha256 ,
.BR sha512 ,
.BR sha3\-256 ,
.B sha3\-512
or
.B none
(which removes them). PixInsight opens files with
.BR sha1 ,
.B sha256
and
.B sha512
checksums only. ASDF blocks always carry an MD5 checksum.
.SS Inspection
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Check the files given, and the XISF, FITS, ASDF and DNG files in the directories given, without
converting anything: checksums are verified, compressed data is decompressed, sizes are compared.
The exit status is 1 if a file is damaged.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-info
Print the geometry, the keywords and the properties of the images. Nothing is converted.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-header
Print the header: the XML header of an XISF file and the YAML tree of an ASDF file as they are, and
of a FITS or DNG file what
.B \-\-info
prints. Nothing is converted.
.SS Other
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress warnings.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the options.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and the codecs this build has.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file was converted, or
.B \-\-verify
found no damage.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be read, converted or written, or
.B \-\-verify
found a damaged one, or a pattern matched nothing, or a directory could not be read. The other
files were still processed. What only the file shows ends this way too: a codec its output format
does not have,
.B \-\-in\-place
on a file that is not XISF.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was refused before a file was read: an option that does not exist, a value an
option does not take, options that exclude each other, no input,
.B \-o
with more than one input or with a directory, an output directory that is not there, a directory
with both XISF and FITS files without
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B 130
The run was interrupted (Ctrl-C, or a request to terminate): the file that was being converted
is not written, and no temporary file is left; the files before it are written.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XISFCONV_MEMORY_LIMIT
What the data that a conversion keeps between reading and writing may take in memory together: a
compressed block of the input, decompressed; the pixels of an image that is written to XISF
compressed, and what they compress to. In bytes, or with
.BR K ,
.B M
or
.B G
(1024, 1024\(S2, 1024\(S3); 256M by default. Beyond it that data is kept in temporary files
beside the output.
.B 0
keeps all of it in temporary files. An image itself is never held whole: it is read and written
a piece at a time.
.TP
.B XISFCONV_PIECE_BYTES
About how much of an image one piece holds; 4M by default. What is written does not depend on it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB name .part
An output is written under this name and renamed when it is complete, so that a run that is
interrupted leaves no half-written file under the final name. One that already exists is not
overwritten without
.BR \-\-force ,
and never when it is the input itself.
.TP
.BI .xisfconv\- XXXXXX
A temporary file beside the output, for data that does not fit
.BR XISFCONV_MEMORY_LIMIT .
On Linux and macOS it is removed as soon as it is made, and is not seen; on Windows it is there
while the conversion runs, and the system removes it when it is closed.
.TP
.IB name .replaced
Where a file cannot be renamed over the one that
.B \-\-force
replaces, the old file is set aside under this name until the new one has its name, and put back
if it does not get it. The data blocks file of a distributed unit that is replaced (with
.B \-\-force
or
.BR \-\-in\-place )
is set aside this way on every system, until the header has its name. A run that is killed in
that moment may leave it:
.B xisfconv \-\-verify
on the output then says whether the replacement was complete.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert an XISF file to FITS, and a FITS file to a compressed XISF file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xisfconv M31_integration.xisf
xisfconv \-c light_0001.fits
.fi
.RE
.PP
The raw image of a camera's DNG file as XISF, with its colour filter pattern, and as FITS:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xisfconv IMG_0001.dng
xisfconv \-t fits IMG_0001.dng
.fi
.RE
.PP
A stretched 8-bit PNG to look at, its longest side 1024 pixels, of every XISF file here:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xisfconv \-t png \-s \-b u8 \-\-resize 1024 *.xisf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compress the XISF files of a folder where they are, and check an archive:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xisfconv \-c \-\-in\-place *.xisf
xisfconv \-\-verify ~/astro/2026
.fi
.RE
.PP
A FITS file for every XISF file of a tree, next to it, and on a later day for those that were
added; previews of the whole tree, in the same folders below another one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xisfconv \-t fits lights/
xisfconv \-t fits lights/ \-\-skip\-existing
xisfconv \-t png \-s \-b u8 \-\-resize 1024 lights/ \-d previews/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write a distributed unit, and pack it into one file again:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xisfconv \-t xish light_0001.xisf
xisfconv light_0001.xish \-t xisf \-o packed.xisf
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
In a pattern, brackets are not special
.RI ( "M31 [Ha].xisf"
is a name on Windows), a name that begins with a dot is matched only by a pattern that begins with
one, and letters match as the system compares names: on Windows whatever their case. On Unix, an
argument that is the name of a file is that file, whatever characters it has.
.PP
What a file of a directory is goes by its name; a file that is named on the command line is read
and converted whatever it is called, and whatever it is: to rewrite XISF files into another
directory, name them or give a pattern. A pattern brings along every file it matches, as a shell
does, and one that is no image is an error of its own. On Windows,
.B "xisfconv /?"
shows the help.
.PP
A header of a distributed unit that names a file outside its own directory is refused unless
.B "\-\-external\-files anywhere"
is given. Give that option only for files whose origin is known.
.PP
The full description of what each conversion does, its limitations, and what was verified against
PixInsight is in the file
.I MANUAL.md
of the distribution; the library and the Python package are described in
.IR docs/manual.html .
